g  superiority numbers there and and and  the appearance is surrounded  uh and i think the most likely thing  that that would result in is the freeing  up of those troops to go north um  to  assist the  russian campaign and the donbas  an alternative is that they would go in  the opposite direction towards odessa  but i think it's now looking as if the  russians have given up on on trying to  take odessa  well are the mil ukrainian forces  prepared um in the east and and  southeast of the country  they've been there a long time you know  there have been ukrainian forces in that  area since  the russians invaded the donbas and took  crimea in 2014. so  so they've been there a long time they  will dug in  they're  well equipped or certainly increasingly  well equipped by  by western uh munitions and and  um  and so  you know i think  it's a significant proportion of the  ukrainian army i guess about just under  half uh is in that area so i think it  it's not an easy thing to do for the  russians to  uh  overcome them  the russian armed forces would have to  get considerably more effective  to do so uh  you know i don't think they're going to  succeed even with their more  limited objectives what they can do is a  lot of damage to ukrainian  infrastructure and kill a lot of  civilians  do you think we're going to see an  escalation in the level of force that  the russian military is prepared to use  yes  i think certainly in terms of artillery  munitions and missiles  friends will fire an enormous amount of  ordnance  uh against the ukrainians not just the  military but against the cities which  will which will destroy them and and  kill civilians  so so yes i think the the volume of  uh  of munitions fired by the russians is  going to increase and and uh  it's it's going to be horrible  military analyst and former british army  general chris deveral thank you  thank you pablo  earlier we spoke with dr aglais netkoff  from london she is a lecturer in  international politics at university  college london school of slavonic and  east european studies she explained the  potential importance of what happened to  the warship  i think it's significant that the  russians have admitted there is a fire  on board  and that they've had to evacuate i think  that's significant whether or not it was  hit by  the ukrainian missiles most likely it  was but at this stage we cannot  independently verify i think  symbolically it is huge  my sky is a major warship um  it was it was most notably involved in  the sort of snake island incident the  stamps of which have actually uh only  come out this week  in ukrainian  in ukraine  symbolically it's massive it will also  be important strategically militarily  because the russians are no longer able  to move their large warships into the  area because the straits have been shut  down and also because it shows that um  the russians are  suffer ongoing suffering heavy losses  which i think before the campaign in  dambass is renewed i think it's you know  it's symbolically important that the  russians are suffering such heavy losses  even before their campaign in dambassa  sort of you know is stepped up again  um  i think there's also a question of how  long can the russians continue to suffer  such sort of heavy losses because um  such warships are sort of very expensive  very difficult to produce there's not  many of them around  that was dr aglais netkov a lecturer in  international politics at university  college london school of slavonic and  east european studies
 

